{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016Yq9VvpsCvGmHvi3LTaDTS","type":"tool_result","content":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-challenger-5eu0mlru\ntotal 16\ndrwx------@     4 brenn  staff     128 Oct  2 16:45 .\ndrwx------@ 13067 brenn  staff  418144 Oct  2 16:46 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x@    13 brenn  staff     416 Oct  2 12:20 agent\n-rw-r--r--@     1 brenn  staff    7143 Oct  2 16:45 sheet.md\n# Gap hunt: E06 — Enforced gateway boundary\n\nA reviewer couldn't decide whether this item is met. **Your job is to find out whether any\ncheck below is false in this codebase.** The reviewer's partial answers and the code they\nlooked at are shown. Read the code, and open the repo files the checks point to.\n\nFor each check, decide:\n- **fails**: you found the line that makes it false (a missing control, a bypass, a\n  value never used, an exception that continues, a filter that lets something through).\n  Quote that line;\n- **holds**: you found the code that makes it true in every case;\n- **unclear**: you can't tell from this repo.\n\n**The requirement for E06:** Send MCP calls through a gateway, or a similar enforcement layer, that the agent can't get around. Authenticate each caller (prove who they are), authorize each action, and validate calls and responses. Enforce rate limits and log every decision. For HTTP and OAuth, check the token's issuer, intended audience, expiry, and scopes. Use properly authorized credentials for the services behind the gateway. Never pass an unchecked token through. For local tools that talk over standard input/output (stdio), control process launch, file access, credentials, and networking in the host or sandbox. An HTTP gateway alone doesn't cover these. Test going around the gateway straight to the server, bad credentials, broken responses, and outages of the policy engine or scanner. MCP copies some request details into headers. If routing or policy uses those headers, reject any request whose headers don't match its body, before checking policy. Store any cached tool list or response separately for each authorization context. When the gateway is itself an OAuth client, check the issuer (`iss`, RFC 9207). Also protect discovery URLs against SSRF (tricking a server into calling places it shouldn't). Required checks must fail closed: if a required check can't run, block the action.\n\nIf a check fails, also copy the words from this requirement that the code breaks into\n`violates`. Then write your answer (format at the end).\n\n\n## E06.1\n\n**Check:** The agent can reach MCP servers only through the gateway: network policy blocks direct routes, the agent holds no server credentials, and any local stdio servers are launched by the host or sandbox with limited files, credentials, and network.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Find how the agent reaches MCP servers: URLs in `*.mcp.json` and client config. 2) Check all go to the gateway, and NetworkPolicy or egress rules block direct routes. 3) Check the agent holds no server credentials and stdio servers run in a restricted sandbox. 4) Answer no if any tool call bypasses the gateway. Answer unknown if network policy lives outside this repo.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** The MCP gateway's token checks, authorization, logging and fail-closed behaviour are outside this repo\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## E06.2\n\n**Check:** The gateway checks each token's issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes, and uses its own separately authorized credentials downstream instead of passing the caller's token through.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Open the gateway's token validation. 2) Check issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes are each verified. 3) Check downstream calls use the gateway's own credentials. 4) Answer no if any claim is unchecked, signature verification is disabled, or the caller's `Authorization` header is forwarded downstream.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** The MCP gateway's token checks, authorization, logging and fail-closed behaviour are outside this repo\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## E06.3\n\n**Check:** The gateway authorizes each action, validates requests and responses, enforces rate limits, and logs every decision.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Open the gateway's request handling. 2) Check per-action authorization, request and response validation, rate limits, and a log entry for every decision. 3) Answer no if any is missing, authorization happens only once per connection, or denials are not logged.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** The MCP gateway's token checks, authorization, logging and fail-closed behaviour are outside this repo\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## E06.4\n\n**Check:** If a required check (policy engine, scanner, token check) errors or times out, the call is blocked.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the policy engine, scanner, and token check. 2) Check each error blocks the call. 3) Answer no if any error branch allows the call or logs and continues, or a config flag defaults to fail-open.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** The MCP gateway's token checks, authorization, logging and fail-closed behaviour are outside this repo\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## E06.5\n\n**Check:** Requests whose routing headers (such as Mcp-Name) don't match the body are rejected before policy, and cached tool lists and responses are stored per authorization context.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Find where routing or policy reads headers such as `Mcp-Name`. 2) Check the header is compared with the body before policy runs, and mismatches are rejected. 3) Check tool-list and response caches are keyed by authorization context. 4) Answer no if headers are trusted without the compare, or a cache is shared across callers.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** The MCP gateway's token checks, authorization, logging and fail-closed behaviour are outside this repo\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## E06.6\n\n**Check:** If the gateway is itself an OAuth client, it checks the authorization server's issuer (iss, RFC 9207) and blocks private and metadata addresses in discovery URLs. Answer yes with the code line, or yes citing the config that shows it isn't an OAuth client.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Check whether the gateway acts as an OAuth client (gets tokens, follows discovery or metadata URLs). 2) If so, check it verifies the `iss` parameter (RFC 9207) and blocks private, loopback, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254) in discovery URLs. 3) Answer no if either is missing. If it is not an OAuth client, answer yes citing the config that shows that.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** The MCP gateway's token checks, authorization, logging and fail-closed behaviour are outside this repo\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## Your answer\n\nWrite this JSON to E06.verdict.json in this folder (or put it in verdicts.json under \"E06\"):\n```json\n{\"verdict\": \"upheld | failed | unclear\",\n \"checks\": [{\"id\": \"E06.1\", \"result\": \"holds | fails | unclear\", \"evidence\": \"path:line\", \"quote\": \"exact code\", \"note\": \"why\"}],\n \"note\": \"one sentence\"}\n```\nVerdict is **failed** if any check fails, **unclear** if none fails but any is unclear, otherwise **upheld**. A failed check needs the quoted line that makes it false: `evidence` is path:line, and `quote` is the exact text of that line. To cite two places, list both refs in `evidence` (`a.py:12, b.yaml:40`) and put each line's text on its own line in `quote`. If the check fails, add `\"violates\": [\"words copied exactly from the requirement\"]`.\nagent/migrations/001_init.sql\nagent/app/killswitch.py\nagent/app/ingest.py\nagent/app/startup.py\nagent/app/tools/payments.py\nagent/app/tools/erp.py\nagent/app/tools/memory.py\nagent/app/tools/documents.py\nagent/app/tools/__init__.py\nagent/app/tools/slack.py\nag